cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use ARGUS, please cite the following paper."
type: software
title: "ARGUS: Evidence-Constrained Agentic Reasoning for Noncoding Regulatory Variant Interpretation"
authors:
  - family-names: Dutta
    given-names: Pratik
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6943-7098"
    affiliation: "Department of Biomedical Informatics, Stony Brook University"
  - family-names: Davuluri
    given-names: Ramana V.
    affiliation: "Department of Biomedical Informatics, Stony Brook University"
abstract: >-
  ARGUS is an evidence-constrained agentic framework for interpreting noncoding
  regulatory variant predictions. It wraps 458 DNABERT-based TF binding models
  in a hypothesis-directed investigation loop where a planner selects evidence
  sources based on current uncertainty, a verifier deterministically interprets
  each observation, and intermediate results change the investigation path.
  Deterministic classification prevents LLM hallucination structurally.
keywords:
  - noncoding regulatory variants
  - transcription factor binding
  - evidence-constrained reasoning
  - agentic AI
  - DNABERT
  - variant interpretation
  - hallucination prevention
